\section{Data, intensity prior, and reproducibility}{

These controls are accepted by every backend.
\describe{
\item{\code{predict_at}}{Numeric matrix with \code{ncol(x)} columns, default \code{x}.
Rows specify locations at which posterior intensity draws are stored.
Values must be finite and inside \code{region}; zero rows are allowed.
Saved tree states also support later evaluation with \code{\link[=ppt_lambda]{ppt_lambda()}}.}
\item{\code{test}}{Optional matrix of held-out event locations, with the same
columns and domain as \code{x}. Default \code{NULL}; zero rows are allowed.
These points are used for posterior predictive scoring, not fitting.
The score treats them as a point pattern with the same observation
exposure as the fitted data; use \code{\link[=ppt_lppd]{ppt_lppd()}} to specify another scale.}
\item{\code{a}}{Positive scalar leaf-intensity prior shape, default \code{0.5}.
With positive \code{b}, leaf intensities have a Gamma(shape = \code{a},
rate = \code{b}) prior, with mean \code{a / b}.}
\item{\code{b}}{Leaf-intensity prior rate. Soft backends require a positive
scalar; their default \code{NULL} sets
\code{a * prod(region[, 2] - region[, 1]) / nrow(x)}.
Hard backends accept a nonnegative scalar, default \code{0}.
Their \code{b = 0} convention uses the unnormalized leaf prior density
\eqn{p(\lambda)\propto\lambda^{a-1}} and leaf score
\eqn{\Gamma(a+m)/H^{a+m}}, for count \eqn{m} and exposure \eqn{H};
it does not define absolute Bayesian evidence.
The quadrature wrapper instead defaults to
\code{a * sum(weights) / nrow(x)}.}
\item{\code{seed}}{Seed passed to \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} once before fitting,
default \code{1L}. Subsequent chains continue the resulting RNG stream.
For reproducibility, use a non-missing integer seed and the same R,
package, and RNG settings.}
}
}

\section{Tree prior and candidate support}{

\describe{
\item{\code{max_depth}}{Integer maximum leaf depth, with root depth zero.
Default \code{8L}, except soft PGAS uses \code{6L}. Values \code{0:20} are accepted
by hard backends and local soft samplers; \code{0} forces a root-only tree.
Soft PGAS requires \code{1:19}. Dense hard SMC and hard Particle Gibbs
additionally require
\eqn{(2P+3)(2^{D+1}-1)\le 2^{24}}, where \eqn{P} is \code{particles}
and \eqn{D} is \code{max_depth}. These are tree-storage guards, not total
memory limits.}
\item{\code{min_leaf_n}}{Positive integer, default \code{1L}, accepted by every
backend except soft PGAS. A candidate split must place at least this
many observations in each child under deterministic routing.
For soft gates this restricts candidate support, not latent label counts.}
\item{\code{cut_candidates}}{Integer requested candidate count, default \code{50L}
for every backend. At least \code{1L} for hard SMC and hard Particle Gibbs;
at least \code{2L} otherwise. Node-specific quantile grids request this
many probability levels before duplicate and invalid cuts are removed.
The local soft \code{"uniform"} rule uses at least 30 positions and its
\code{"data"} rule ignores this count. Soft PGAS uses a global grid per
coordinate unless \code{cut_grid} is supplied. Matching candidate counts
across backend families does not imply matching tree priors or support.}
\item{\code{alpha}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1)}, default \code{0.95}, accepted by hard local
samplers and every soft backend. Base split probability in
\eqn{P(\mathrm{split}\mid h)=\alpha(1+h)^{-\eta}}, before depth and
candidate-support restrictions, where \eqn{h} is node depth. The
computed probability is clipped to \verb{[1e-12, 1 - 1e-12]} for numerical
stability. Hard SMC
and hard Particle Gibbs fix the base value at \code{0.5}; they do not accept
this control.}
\item{\code{eta}}{Nonnegative scalar depth penalty, default \code{2}, accepted by
the same backends as \code{alpha}. Larger values reduce split probabilities
more rapidly with depth. Hard SMC and hard Particle Gibbs fix it at \code{2}.}
\item{\code{cut_proposal}}{Character scalar for local soft samplers only:
\itemize{
\item \code{"quantile"} (default): node-specific empirical cut locations
at probabilities from 0.05 to 0.95.
\item \code{"uniform"}: equally spaced locations inside the node's box,
using \code{max(30, cut_candidates)} positions before count filtering.
\item \code{"data"}: midpoints between adjacent distinct observed values
in the node, subject to boundary and child-count restrictions.
}
Node observations here are those routed by the hard split rules.
Quantile and data rules exclude cuts within \code{1e-3} of a node boundary,
in the units of the corresponding coordinate. All rules enforce
\code{min_leaf_n}.}
\item{\code{max_aspect_ratio}}{Numeric scalar in \verb{[1, Inf]}, default \code{Inf};
hard SMC and hard Particle Gibbs only. Maximum ratio of longest to
shortest side of each candidate child box, in coordinate units.
\code{Inf} removes this ratio restriction; other shape and count checks
still apply.}
}
}

\section{MCMC length and output}{

All samplers other than hard SMC accept \code{chains}, \code{iter}, \code{burn}, and
\code{verbose}. Defaults differ by backend:
\tabular{lrrrr}{
Backend \tab chains \tab iter \tab burn \tab thin\cr
Hard RJ-MCMC / informed MH \tab 4 \tab 4000 \tab 1000 \tab --\cr
Soft local samplers \tab 4 \tab 10000 \tab 2500 \tab 3\cr
Hard Particle Gibbs \tab 1 \tab 500 \tab 100 \tab --\cr
Soft PGAS \tab 1 \tab 1000 \tab 200 \tab 1
}
\describe{
\item{\code{chains}}{Positive integer number of chains, run sequentially.}
\item{\code{iter}}{Integer total iterations per chain, including warm-up;
must be greater than \code{burn}.}
\item{\code{burn}}{Nonnegative integer number of initial iterations discarded.}
\item{\code{thin}}{Positive integer retention interval for soft backends only.
Output is saved at one-based iteration numbers
\code{burn + 1}, \code{burn + 1 + thin}, and so on, not exceeding \code{iter}.
Each chain retains \code{ceiling((iter - burn) / thin)} states.}
\item{\code{prediction_draws}}{Positive integer requested number of retained
intensity draws and tree states per chain; default \code{300L}, hard local
samplers only. With \code{K = iter - burn}, retention uses interval
\code{max(1, floor(K / min(prediction_draws, K)))}, starting at the first
post-warm-up state. Thus the actual count can exceed the requested
value when the interval does not divide \code{K}. Structural diagnostics
are recorded at every post-warm-up iteration.}
\item{\code{verbose}}{Logical scalar, default \code{TRUE}. Displays chain messages
and an RcppProgress bar counting completed iterations, including
warm-up and states discarded by thinning. \code{FALSE} suppresses them.
Not accepted by hard SMC.}
}
}

\section{Soft gates and their priors}{

Every soft backend accepts the following controls except where noted.
The controls \code{gate}, \code{a_gate}, \code{b_gate}, \code{sd_gate}, and \code{gate_min} must be
numeric scalars or vectors of length \code{ncol(x)} for dimension-specific
gates. A scalar is recycled across coordinates. Shared gates require
scalar controls. All values must be finite.
\describe{
\item{\code{gate_structure}}{Character scalar:
\itemize{
\item \code{"dimension"} (default): one gate parameter per coordinate,
shared across all splits on that coordinate.
\item \code{"shared"}: one common gate parameter for every split.
}}
\item{\code{gate}}{Positive initial gate sharpness, default \code{12}; every entry
must exceed its \code{gate_min}. Larger values sharpen the transition at
a fixed cut and width. If \code{update_gate = FALSE}, this is the fixed value.}
\item{\code{a_gate}}{Positive gate-prior shape, default \code{36}. Each free gate
\eqn{\gamma} has density proportional to
\eqn{\gamma^{a_g-1}\exp(-b_g\gamma)\,1\{\gamma>g_{\min}\}}, where
\eqn{a_g}, \eqn{b_g}, and \eqn{g_{\min}} are the corresponding
\code{a_gate}, \code{b_gate}, and \code{gate_min}. Dimension-specific gates have
independent priors; a shared gate contributes one prior factor.}
\item{\code{b_gate}}{Positive gate-prior rate, default \code{3}. With
\code{gate_min = 0}, the prior mean is \code{a_gate / b_gate}. A positive lower
bound gives a truncated Gamma distribution, whose mean differs.}
\item{\code{gate_min}}{Nonnegative lower truncation bound, default \code{0}.
Gate proposals at or below the bound have zero target density.}
\item{\code{sd_gate}}{Positive log-gate proposal standard deviation,
default \code{0.07}. RJ-MCMC, informed MH, and PGAS use coordinate-wise
Gaussian random walks in log gates, or one random walk for a shared
gate. PCG uses these as the initial diagonal proposal standard
deviations before joint RAM adaptation.}
\item{\code{update_gate}}{Logical scalar, default \code{TRUE}. Enables gate
Metropolis updates. \code{FALSE} fixes gates at \code{gate} and disables PCG
adaptation; tree and allocation updates still run.}
\item{\code{gate_family}}{Character scalar for local soft samplers only:
\itemize{
\item \code{"logistic"} (default): right-routing probability
\eqn{\{1+\exp[-\gamma_j(x_j-c)/w]\}^{-1}}.
\item \code{"compact"}: right-routing probability \eqn{t^2(3-2t)} for
\eqn{0<t<1}, with values zero below and one above this interval;
\eqn{t=(x_j-c+h)/(2h)}, \eqn{h=w/\gamma_j}, and \eqn{w} is the
splitting node's width.
}
Soft PGAS uses logistic gates and does not accept \code{gate_family}.}
\item{\code{gate_scale}}{\code{NULL} (default) or a character scalar:
\itemize{
\item \code{"root"}: logistic gates use \code{region[j, 2] - region[j, 1]}
for \eqn{w}, regardless of node depth.
\item \code{"node"}: use the splitting node's width for \eqn{w}.
Supported by local soft samplers; soft PGAS rejects this value.
\item \code{NULL}: resolves to \code{"root"} for logistic gates and \code{"node"}
for compact gates. Compact gates reject \code{"root"}.
}
This controls width scaling independently of parameter sharing.
Node-scaled logistic exposures use deterministic adaptive integration.}
}
}

\section{Local soft tree updates and geometry}{

These controls are accepted by soft RJ-MCMC, informed MH, and PCG.
\describe{
\item{\code{tree_moves}}{Nonnegative integer number of grow/prune tree kernels
per iteration, default \code{3L}. Zero disables these kernels.}
\item{\code{change_moves}}{Nonnegative integer number of terminal-split change
kernels per iteration, default \code{8L}. Zero disables these kernels.}
\item{\code{cache_geometry}}{Logical scalar, default \code{TRUE}. Reuses unchanged
leaf exposures and ancestor routing factors within a fit. Supports
logistic root/node scaling and compact gates. \code{FALSE} uses uncached
calculations for numerical comparisons. No saved files are required;
rounding can differ because multiplication is shared across leaves.}
}
}

\section{Additional PCG controls}{

Accepted only with \verb{gating = "soft", sampler = "pcg"}.
\describe{
\item{\code{ram_target}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1)}, default \code{0.234}. Target acceptance
rate for the joint log-gate robust adaptive Metropolis (RAM) proposal.}
\item{\code{ram_decay}}{Scalar in \verb{(0.5, 1]}, default \code{0.7}. Exponent
\eqn{\kappa} in the adaptation gain
\eqn{\eta_t=\min\{1,r(t+1)^{-\kappa}\}}, where \eqn{r} is the number
of free gate parameters and \eqn{t=0,1,\ldots} indexes gate steps.}
\item{\code{ram_adapt}}{Integer in \verb{[0, burn]}; default \code{NULL} resolves to
\code{floor(0.8 * burn)}. Number of initial iterations in which the RAM
factor can adapt. Zero fixes the initial proposal covariance.}
\item{\code{informed}}{Logical scalar, default \code{FALSE}. With \code{TRUE}, propose
grow/prune/change moves using a cached hard-tree surrogate and the
proposal controls below. Acceptance still uses the soft target and
forward/reverse proposal probabilities. This is distinct from the
exact-neighborhood sampler selected by \code{sampler = "irjmcmc"}.}
\item{\code{proposal_temperature}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1]}, default \code{0.5}.
For \code{informed = TRUE}, multiplies the hard surrogate log score
(including its prior terms) before exponentiating. Smaller values
flatten the informed weights; \code{0.5} takes their square root.}
\item{\code{proposal_defensive}}{Scalar in \verb{[0, 1)}, default \code{0.1}.
For \code{informed = TRUE}, weight of a uniform proposal over the
applicable candidate moves, mixed with the informed proposal.
This is a uniform mixture, unlike the prior-action mixture in PGAS.
Both proposal controls are unused when \code{informed = FALSE}.}
}
}

\section{Hard SMC and Particle Gibbs}{

\describe{
\item{\code{particles}}{Integer at least 2. Defaults: \code{1000L} for hard SMC,
\code{500L} for hard Particle Gibbs, and \code{50L} for soft PGAS. Particle Gibbs
uses this many particles per conditional-SMC sweep, including the
retained reference particle.}
\item{\code{engine}}{Character scalar for hard SMC only:
\itemize{
\item \code{"shared"} (default): particles visiting the same box share
its candidate geometry and scores.
\item \code{"dense"}: separate preallocated tree storage per particle.
}
Both implementations use the hard SMC target and proposal rules.
The quadrature wrapper requires \code{"shared"}.}
\item{\code{resample_thresh}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1]}, default \code{0.5}, hard SMC
only. Resample when particle ESS is below
\code{resample_thresh * particles}. Hard Particle Gibbs does not expose
this control. Soft PGAS instead uses \code{ess_threshold}.}
}
}

\section{Soft PGAS proposal and resampling}{

These controls are accepted only with \verb{gating = "soft", sampler = "pgas"}.
\describe{
\item{\code{cut_grid}}{Default \code{NULL}, or a list of \code{ncol(x)} nonempty numeric
vectors of finite cuts strictly inside the respective root interval.
Supplied vectors are sorted and deduplicated and override automatic
cut generation. The automatic grid uses type-1 sample quantiles at
\code{cut_candidates} probabilities from 0.05 to 0.95; cuts within 0.001
times root width of a boundary are removed. A coordinate with no
remaining cut uses its region midpoint. The grid stays fixed across
nodes and iterations.}
\item{\code{label_sweeps}}{Nonnegative integer, default \code{1L}. Additional Gibbs
sweeps over observation labels per iteration after the conditional-SMC
tree/allocation update. Zero omits these additional sweeps.}
\item{\code{ancestor_sampling}}{Logical scalar, default \code{TRUE}. Enables
ancestor sampling for the retained reference trajectory.
\code{FALSE} uses conditional SMC without ancestor sampling.}
\item{\code{exact_max}}{Nonnegative integer, default \code{150L}. Largest allocated
node count at which candidate scores and child allocations use the
exact Poisson-binomial recursion. Above it, \code{proposal_score} chooses
the action scorer and \code{allocation} chooses the allocation proposal.
Zero still allows exact scoring of empty nodes.}
\item{\code{proposal_score}}{Character scalar for nodes above \code{exact_max}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"laplace"} (default): Laplace approximation to the integrated
child-allocation score.
\item \code{"hard"}: surrogate using cumulative hard-split counts and
hard-threshold exposures under the current soft parent path.
True logistic gates are evaluated lazily; selected splits use
true soft exposures and the full importance correction.
}
Exact-scored nodes are unaffected. Runtime and mixing gains are
data-dependent.}
\item{\code{proposal_temperature}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1]}, default \code{0.5}.
For \code{proposal_score = "hard"}, tempers only the surrogate likelihood
ratio: the informed action weight is
\eqn{p_0(A)\widetilde L(A)^{\tau}}, with \eqn{\tau} this control.
The prior action weight \eqn{p_0} is not tempered. Smaller values
reduce concentration on the highest surrogate scores.}
\item{\code{proposal_defensive}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1)}, default \code{0.1}.
For the hard scorer, the action proposal is
\eqn{(1-\epsilon)q_{\mathrm{informed}}+\epsilon p_0}, with
\eqn{\epsilon} this control. Thus 0.1 assigns 10 percent of the mixture
to the prior action distribution. This and \code{proposal_temperature}
affect only hard-scored large nodes, but their ranges are validated
for every soft PGAS call. They do not change the posterior target.}
\item{\code{defensive}}{Scalar in \verb{[0, 1)}, default \code{0}. Prior-action mixture
weight for exact and Laplace scoring. It is separate from the hard
scorer's \code{proposal_defensive}.}
\item{\code{allocation}}{Character scalar for nodes above \code{exact_max}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"sequential"} (default): allocate observations to children
one at a time using sequential imputation.
\item \code{"rates"}: propose auxiliary child rates and allocate
observations independently conditional on those rates.
}
Both branches include their corresponding importance correction.}
\item{\code{resampling}}{Character scalar determining resampling opportunities:
\itemize{
\item \code{"node"} (default): after each heap node at which at least
one particle advanced, except the final heap position.
\item \code{"level"}: after each tree level except the final level.
}
The final tree is selected using the final particle weights.}
\item{\code{ess_threshold}}{Scalar in \verb{(0, 1]}, default \code{1}. At a resampling
opportunity, resample if ESS is at most
\code{ess_threshold * particles}. The default therefore resamples at
every such opportunity.}
}

